              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">8.8 mcg</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">FDA interim reference level, women of childbearing age</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-check" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Check Any Protein Powder for Heavy Metals
          </h2>

          <p>
            First, look for the product in a published test, and match the exact product and flavor. A good result for a brand's whey says nothing about the same brand's plant powder or mass gainer, and a chocolate result may not apply to vanilla. Note the year, because formulas and suppliers change: Vega told CR its tested powder has since been renamed and its pea sourcing changed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Second, ask for a certificate of analysis. A CoA is a lab report for a specific lot, and reputable brands will share one for the lot number printed on your tub, listing lead, cadmium, arsenic and mercury in micrograms per serving or parts per million. Check that the lab is independent and the date is recent. A brand that will not share a CoA, or only shares one with no numbers, is telling you something.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third, understand what certifications cover. NSF Certified for Sport is built on the NSF/ANSI 173 standard, which includes heavy-metal contaminant limits, but those limits are looser than CR's level of concern: Garden of Life Sport's plant powder is NSF certified, per the brand, yet measured about 564% of CR's level. Informed Choice and Informed Sport focus on banned substances. The Clean Label Project's certification is about contaminants specifically; check its site for the exact product.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, manage dose. CR assumed one serving a day; two servings double the exposure. Rotating between a dairy powder and whole-food protein, and keeping plant or chocolate powders to one serving, keeps total intake down. Pregnant people, children and anyone with kidney disease should talk to a doctor or registered dietitian before relying on protein powder. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a> guide covers the other risks, and our <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">best clean protein powder</a> guide ranks short-label options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every product here had to have a published, citable basis for a low heavy-metal claim: an independent test result from Consumer Reports, a contaminant-inclusive certification from NSF, or lot-level testing the brand documents. We name the source and year for each, and we did not run any lab tests of our own. Within that pool we ranked on the strength of the evidence first, then protein per serving, ingredients and cost per gram. We excluded products CR placed in its avoid or limit groups, and we left out powders with no public test data at all, however clean their marketing sounds.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming any powder is heavy-metal free</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lead and cadmium occur naturally in soil, so trace amounts appear in nearly every food. Look for powders with published low results, not claims of zero. A brand promising no heavy metals at all is overstating what testing can show.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating one test as a permanent verdict</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Consumer Reports and the Clean Label Project tested specific products, flavors and lots at a point in time. Formulas, suppliers and harvests change. Match the exact product and flavor, note the year, and ask for a recent certificate of analysis.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Defaulting to chocolate plant protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The Clean Label Project's 2025 report found chocolate averaged about four times the lead of vanilla, and plant powders roughly three times whey. Chocolate plant protein combines both risks. Vanilla or unflavored dairy is the lowest-risk default.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading a Prop 65 warning as proof of danger</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Prop 65's 0.5 microgram lead threshold is deliberately cautious, and some companies warn defensively. A warning is a prompt to look for test data, not proof a product is unsafe, and a missing warning does not prove it is clean.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring how many servings you take</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Consumer Reports' percentages assume one serving a day. Two shakes double your exposure, and adding a plant-based bar or chocolate snack adds more. Count servings across the day, especially for higher-testing plant or chocolate powders.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

