            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is there lead in protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Most protein powders contain trace lead because it is present in soil and water. Consumer Reports' October 2025 investigation found more than two-thirds of 23 products exceeded its 0.5 microgram daily level of concern in one serving. Dairy-based powders tested lowest; plant-based products averaged about nine times more lead than dairy, per CR.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best protein powder without lead?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No product can promise zero lead, but CR's October 2025 testing found no detectable lead in MuscleTech 100% Mass Gainer and low levels in Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Whey, BSN Syntha-6. For everyday use, a vanilla or unflavored whey with published results or lot-level certificates of analysis is the safest choice.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Orgain protein powder have lead?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Consumer Reports measured Orgain Organic Plant-Based Protein Powder in Vanilla Bean at about 143% of its daily lead level of concern per serving in October 2025, and rated it okay to have occasionally, at about four to five servings a week. Orgain has said its products are safe and comply with food safety standards. CR did not test Orgain's whey powder.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is whey or plant protein lower in heavy metals?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Whey and other dairy proteins consistently test lower. Consumer Reports found plant-based powders averaged about nine times more lead than dairy-based ones in 2025, and the Clean Label Project's 2025 report found plant powders had roughly three times more lead than whey. Plants absorb metals from soil, and isolating the protein concentrates them.
